<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
        The window around Compose, and the only Android-side styling this app has.

        DayNight Material, so the window follows the phone before a single frame is composed.
        Which set of system-bar icons is drawn is then corrected from Compose (`SystemBars`),
        because the theme is a setting *in the app* and may disagree with the phone.

        The window background is the launch screen's felt (below), so the frames between the
        launch screen and the first composition, and a rotation or a resize, are the green every
        screen of the app is laid on (`feltGradient`) rather than a flash of white. It was the
        rail until the launch screen existed; the felt is what the launch screen hands over to.

        Like everything here it follows the *system* setting rather than the app's, which is
        the best that can be done: the app's own choice lives in a file that has not been read
        yet. A player who overrides the theme in Settings goes from one felt to the other on a
        cold start — a step between two greens rather than between paper and slate.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Vinto" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.DayNight">
        <!-- The DayNight parent brings an action bar with it, which this app has no use
             for: the table draws its own header. -->
        <item name="android:windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/launch_felt</item>
        <!-- Ignored from Android 15 on, where edge-to-edge makes the bars transparent and
             the rail behind them shows through anyway. Kept for older phones. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/rail</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/rail</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The launch screen: the app icon's own V standing whole on the icon's own felt, with no
        disk and no frame. Without it Android built one from the adaptive icon — the felt tile
        cropped into a circle on grey, which the owner called a "strange logo inside border".

        AndroidX core-splashscreen, so every Android from 24 shows the same screen.
        `MainActivity` installs it before anything draws, and it hands over to `Theme.Vinto`.

        The mark and both colours are GENERATED by `tools/make-launch-screen.mjs` from
        `brand/vinto-icon.svg` (`drawable/launch_mark.xml`, `values{,-night}/launch.xml`): the
        light icon's felt and V on a light phone, the dark icon's on a dark one — which are also
        the greens the first screen's felt is drawn in. The icon background is the felt too, so
        nothing outlines the mark.

        On Android 12+ the V is written in, one pen stroke down the left arm and up the right
        (`drawable/launch_mark_animated.xml`, generated beside the still). `@drawable/launch_icon`
        chooses (`values{,-v31}/launch_animation.xml`): older Android and the compat library show
        an animated vector's first frame, an empty felt, so they keep the still. The duration is
        the animation's own length, which `MainActivity` holds the screen for.

        The felt is dark green on a light phone as well, so the bars carry the light icon set in
        both themes; the library's DayNight parent would draw dark icons on it by day.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Vinto.Launch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/launch_felt</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/launch_icon</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">@integer/launch_animation_ms</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@color/launch_felt</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Vinto</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="27">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
